WEBVTT

00:00:00.608 --> 00:00:04.696
Picture a robot moving between two ends of a corridor Near either end

00:00:05.091 --> 00:00:08.558
reaching that nearby goal has become routine The interesting learning

00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:12.300
happens around obstacles in the middle A fixed schedule keeps sending

00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:15.839
the robot all the way through mastered segments before turning around

00:00:16.234 --> 00:00:19.880
Those extra interactions may add little new information about the parts

00:00:19.892 --> 00:00:24.459
of the task it still struggles with risk proposes an unusual stopping

00:00:24.471 --> 00:00:28.130
rule switch away from the current goal when the agent is confident it

00:00:28.142 --> 00:00:31.728
can reach it The goal need not have been physically reached yet This

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:35.901
can shift practice toward less familiar state-goal combinations It is

00:00:35.913 --> 00:00:39.775
a curriculum mechanism for allocating experience rather than a failure

00:00:39.787 --> 00:00:43.863
detector that activates whenever progress looks poor Compare this carefully

00:00:43.959 --> 00:00:48.729
with Leave no Trace There low predicted recovery value rejects a potentially

00:00:48.741 --> 00:00:52.794
troublesome action Here high competence on the current goal increases

00:00:52.806 --> 00:00:56.285
the chance of handing off to another goal The two scores answer different

00:00:56.297 --> 00:01:01.056
questions This lesson first fixes the value semantics at a handoff then

00:01:01.068 --> 00:01:04.535
derives the competence score and the controls that prevent excessive

00:01:04.547 --> 00:01:04.953
switching

00:01:06.909 --> 00:01:10.950
At a handoff the behavior controller changes which goal it pursues But

00:01:10.962 --> 00:01:14.465
what should happen to the critic for the old goal If that critic estimates

00:01:14.477 --> 00:01:18.578
the return of continuing toward the old goal its future has not vanished

00:01:18.984 --> 00:01:22.583
The successor state still exists and an imagined continuation of the

00:01:22.595 --> 00:01:26.815
old policy can still have value from that state Off-policy learning

00:01:26.827 --> 00:01:30.928
separates the policy collecting transitions from the policy being evaluated

00:01:31.011 --> 00:01:34.981
or improved A goal-conditioned critic can therefore bootstrap using

00:01:34.993 --> 00:01:38.986
the original goal even if the next real action is collected for a different

00:01:38.998 --> 00:01:43.433
goal The update must use an appropriate off-policy method and goal-consistent

00:01:43.445 --> 00:01:47.714
reward We are not claiming that an arbitrary on-policy trajectory can

00:01:47.726 --> 00:01:51.348
be relabeled without correction The important modeling choice is the

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:55.712
return being estimated If you intentionally define a hierarchical option

00:01:55.724 --> 00:01:59.442
whose return ends at handoff that is a different objective and may have

00:01:59.454 --> 00:02:03.208
a different target risk's argument concerns critics for the underlying

00:02:03.268 --> 00:02:07.166
goal-conditioned control task Treating the scheduler's handoff as their

00:02:07.178 --> 00:02:11.099
terminal event changes those critics into something the method did not

00:02:11.111 --> 00:02:11.864
intend to learn

00:02:13.825 --> 00:02:18.452
Suppose the successor value is eight reward is zero and gamma is point

00:02:18.464 --> 00:02:22.947
nine The continuation target is seven point two If a scheduler happens

00:02:22.959 --> 00:02:26.713
to switch goals at that transition and we label it terminal the target

00:02:26.725 --> 00:02:30.766
becomes zero Nothing about the goal-reaching dynamics changed Only the

00:02:30.778 --> 00:02:34.508
behavior schedule changed yet the critic receives a radically different

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:38.502
learning signal Now let the scheduler improve over training It starts

00:02:38.514 --> 00:02:42.495
switching earlier in familiar regions Under the false-terminal convention

00:02:42.877 --> 00:02:47.421
those very regions receive more zeroed continuation targets The critic's

00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:51.199
meaning drifts with the switching policy In a forward-backward system

00:02:51.462 --> 00:02:55.419
the distribution of states also depends on both controllers making this

00:02:55.431 --> 00:02:59.460
coupling more complicated than a fixed time limit in an ordinary episodic

00:02:59.472 --> 00:03:03.979
benchmark risk uses timeout-nonterminal bootstrapping at these handoffs

00:03:04.087 --> 00:03:08.451
to preserve the continuation targets True goal success can still terminate

00:03:08.463 --> 00:03:12.313
the success return under its own definition The distinction is between

00:03:12.324 --> 00:03:16.126
a boundary in behavior collection and a boundary in the modeled task

00:03:16.533 --> 00:03:19.904
The companion target calculator lets you change the switching decision

00:03:20.096 --> 00:03:23.348
while holding reward successor state and goal constant

00:03:25.300 --> 00:03:28.504
risk needs a measure of how well the current policy can achieve its

00:03:28.516 --> 00:03:33.263
goal A useful choice rewards first success and discounts delay The sooner

00:03:33.275 --> 00:03:37.878
the goal is reached the larger the score Failure to ever reach it contributes

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:42.457
zero This naturally combines ability and distance in one value-like

00:03:42.469 --> 00:03:45.864
quantity that can be learned with familiar temporal difference machinery

00:03:46.271 --> 00:03:50.347
There is a small indexing convention worth making explicit In the implemented

00:03:50.359 --> 00:03:54.819
recurrence success on the very next transition receives reward one without

00:03:54.831 --> 00:03:59.733
discount If tau counts transitions until success the corresponding return

00:03:59.828 --> 00:04:04.539
is gamma to tau minus one The paper also describes the intuition using

00:04:04.551 --> 00:04:08.998
gamma to a hitting-time index A one-step shift in the index is bookkeeping

00:04:09.309 --> 00:04:13.063
not a different curriculum principle For stochastic trajectories the

00:04:13.075 --> 00:04:17.642
learned critic estimates an expectation over possible outcomes rather

00:04:17.654 --> 00:04:22.126
than the shortest path over all imaginable actions A policy that sometimes

00:04:22.138 --> 00:04:25.617
succeeds quickly and sometimes fails can share a score with a slower

00:04:26.011 --> 00:04:29.502
more reliable policy The score is therefore a discounted competence

00:04:29.514 --> 00:04:33.448
measure It is not automatically a calibrated probability of eventual

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:36.461
success as the paper explicitly emphasizes

00:04:38.413 --> 00:04:42.108
Let f be a function that tells us whether the successor state satisfies

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:46.209
the current goal If it does the first-success return is one and there

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:50.776
is no additional tail If it does not the score is gamma times the estimated

00:04:50.788 --> 00:04:54.530
continuation under the same goal-conditioned policy Combining those

00:04:54.542 --> 00:04:58.272
two cases gives the target on screen This recurrence explains why the

00:04:58.284 --> 00:05:02.803
score stays between zero and one in the ideal setting The success branch

00:05:02.935 --> 00:05:06.653
is exactly one The other branch discounts an existing score in that

00:05:06.665 --> 00:05:10.825
range Starting with bounded values the true fixed point respects the

00:05:10.837 --> 00:05:15.237
same bounds A neural approximation can still produce poorly calibrated

00:05:15.249 --> 00:05:19.015
estimates unless its representation and training handle that constraint

00:05:19.027 --> 00:05:23.188
appropriately The success critic is trained alongside the ordinary task

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:27.265
critic using the agent's experience It requires a success function for

00:05:27.276 --> 00:05:31.891
the relevant task and reset goals It does not infer those goal specifications

00:05:31.903 --> 00:05:36.076
from nothing At a collector handoff bootstrap the unsatisfied current

00:05:36.088 --> 00:05:41.205
goal at actual first success use the success branch Two different boundaries

00:05:41.217 --> 00:05:44.313
again receive different treatment because they have different meanings

00:05:46.280 --> 00:05:50.369
Suppose we directly use a competence score of point one as the probability

00:05:50.381 --> 00:05:54.757
of switching at every step That number may sound small But with independent

00:05:54.769 --> 00:05:59.455
trials and a constant hazard the expected waiting time is only ten steps

00:05:59.862 --> 00:06:03.437
If both directions have moderately high scores in the same region the

00:06:03.448 --> 00:06:07.418
robot can keep turning around there instead of reaching unfamiliar territory

00:06:07.812 --> 00:06:11.889
This is a useful distinction between a per-step probability and a trajectory-level

00:06:11.901 --> 00:06:16.492
probability Ten percent per step does not mean only ten percent of trajectories

00:06:16.504 --> 00:06:20.808
switch Over many opportunities the chance of at least one switch becomes

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:24.825
large The product of the probabilities of not switching determines how

00:06:24.837 --> 00:06:28.783
much of the trajectory survives to a given time risk therefore adds

00:06:28.795 --> 00:06:32.824
three controls rather than feeding the score directly into an unrestricted

00:06:32.836 --> 00:06:37.068
coin flip It chooses only a portion of trajectories for early switching

00:06:37.415 --> 00:06:41.850
enforces a minimum trajectory length and gradually relaxes an initially

00:06:41.862 --> 00:06:45.963
conservative factor These controls preserve sustained behavior long

00:06:45.975 --> 00:06:50.016
enough to move through the state space We will derive their joint effect

00:06:50.028 --> 00:06:53.172
instead of hiding them inside a single hyperparameter

00:06:55.115 --> 00:06:59.300
At the beginning of a trajectory draw one eligibility coin with probability

00:06:59.312 --> 00:07:03.735
zeta If that coin fails do not perform intelligent early-switch checks

00:07:03.747 --> 00:07:07.693
during that trajectory This is not the same as independently multiplying

00:07:07.705 --> 00:07:12.858
the hazard by zeta at every step An eligible trajectory keeps its eligibility

00:07:13.157 --> 00:07:17.257
and an ineligible one keeps the opportunity to travel farther For an

00:07:17.269 --> 00:07:21.765
eligible trajectory wait until its length reaches the minimum m After

00:07:21.777 --> 00:07:25.650
that multiply the competence score by one minus beta raised to the current

00:07:25.662 --> 00:07:30.265
trajectory length With beta near one this factor starts small and grows

00:07:30.277 --> 00:07:35.263
gradually With beta equal to one exactly the factor remains zero the

00:07:35.275 --> 00:07:38.849
often-stated limit toward the competence score requires beta strictly

00:07:38.861 --> 00:07:43.177
below one Actual goal success triggers a switch and a maximum length

00:07:43.321 --> 00:07:48.055
M also forces a handoff These conditions come before the optional early-switch

00:07:48.067 --> 00:07:52.240
check in the paper's algorithm The ordinary task critic still bootstraps

00:07:52.347 --> 00:07:56.389
through a mere scheduler cutoff The rule controls which behavior collects

00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:00.848
data it does not redefine the goal-reaching task every time the eligibility

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:01.685
coin is drawn

00:08:03.637 --> 00:08:07.403
The switching probability is a hazard the probability of ending this

00:08:07.415 --> 00:08:11.838
segment now conditional on having continued until now To survive several

00:08:11.850 --> 00:08:17.075
steps the segment must avoid every earlier switch Multiply the one-minus-hazard

00:08:17.087 --> 00:08:20.973
factors to obtain the survival curve If the competence score changes

00:08:20.984 --> 00:08:24.631
along the path use the score at each corresponding state rather than

00:08:24.643 --> 00:08:29.150
a single constant Now include trajectory eligibility Before the maximum

00:08:29.162 --> 00:08:33.789
cutoff an ineligible trajectory survives every intelligent-switch opportunity

00:08:34.172 --> 00:08:38.368
An eligible trajectory survives according to the product The unconditional

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:43.079
survival probability is therefore one minus zeta plus zeta times the

00:08:43.174 --> 00:08:47.072
eligible survival curve This mixture is why replacing the trajectory

00:08:47.084 --> 00:08:50.635
coin with a per-step factor produces a different length distribution

00:08:51.041 --> 00:08:55.082
Summing the tail probabilities gives expected segment length The laboratory

00:08:55.094 --> 00:08:59.661
evaluates this finite sum exactly for a fixed competence a chosen minimum

00:08:59.673 --> 00:09:03.894
length and a forced maximum It deliberately excludes early physical

00:09:03.906 --> 00:09:07.743
goal hits so that the switching mechanism can be isolated You can see

00:09:07.755 --> 00:09:11.868
how beta zeta and the minimum length affect both the mean duration and

00:09:11.880 --> 00:09:13.267
the chance of long segments

00:09:15.217 --> 00:09:19.043
Return to the corridor picture As a robot approaches a goal it already

00:09:19.055 --> 00:09:23.300
knows how to reach the success critic rises The scheduler may switch

00:09:23.311 --> 00:09:27.472
to the opposite goal before arrival The physical state is unchanged

00:09:27.687 --> 00:09:31.681
but the state-goal pair is new Repeating this mechanism can reallocate

00:09:31.692 --> 00:09:35.686
experience away from routinely completed tails and toward portions of

00:09:35.698 --> 00:09:39.930
the problem that remain useful to learn The intended result is a curriculum

00:09:40.002 --> 00:09:44.234
that depends on competence rather than only on a clock or distance threshold

00:09:44.652 --> 00:09:49.076
But competence itself is learned and the induced data distribution changes

00:09:49.232 --> 00:09:53.583
as both policy and critic change A misleading success critic can schedule

00:09:53.595 --> 00:09:58.127
poor practice Frequent handoffs can also produce coverage problems despite

00:09:58.139 --> 00:10:01.857
correct Bellman targets which is why the modulation mechanisms matter

00:10:02.311 --> 00:10:06.424
Think of target correctness and data usefulness as two separate requirements

00:10:06.818 --> 00:10:10.608
Correct bootstrapping gives the critic a consistent quantity to estimate

00:10:11.003 --> 00:10:15.104
Intelligent switching tries to improve the examples available for estimating

00:10:15.116 --> 00:10:19.169
it and improving the policy Fixing either requirement alone does not

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:23.102
automatically solve the other This separation makes the paper's two

00:10:23.114 --> 00:10:27.239
major contributions easier to understand and evaluate independently

00:10:29.185 --> 00:10:33.345
An ablation should isolate a question Removing timeout-aware bootstrapping

00:10:33.357 --> 00:10:37.566
tests whether the target's meaning matters Removing intelligent switching

00:10:37.721 --> 00:10:41.033
while retaining consistent targets tests whether the learned schedule

00:10:41.045 --> 00:10:45.444
improves experience allocation Removing the eligibility coin minimum

00:10:45.456 --> 00:10:49.163
length or conservative factor tests whether the raw competence signal

00:10:49.175 --> 00:10:53.132
causes excessive switching These changes are related but they are not

00:10:53.192 --> 00:10:57.508
interchangeable explanations The paper studies these components empirically

00:10:57.568 --> 00:11:01.585
in its chosen environments Our laboratory does not reproduce those learning

00:11:01.597 --> 00:11:06.092
curves It exposes the mechanics that make the ablations meaningful You

00:11:06.104 --> 00:11:09.667
can compare correct and false-terminal targets without training a neural

00:11:09.679 --> 00:11:13.182
network and compare segment-length distributions without changing the

00:11:13.194 --> 00:11:17.330
physical task Those controlled examples help explain what the empirical

00:11:17.342 --> 00:11:21.479
experiments are testing When applying the idea elsewhere examine goal

00:11:21.491 --> 00:11:26.166
definitions success detection and reset feasibility A high score on

00:11:26.178 --> 00:11:30.577
an easy but irrelevant goal does not necessarily create useful exploration

00:11:30.589 --> 00:11:34.594
after switching Likewise a controller that cannot reach the alternate

00:11:34.606 --> 00:11:39.138
goal may accumulate unhelpful data risk supplies a scheduling mechanism

00:11:39.281 --> 00:11:42.724
whose success depends on the surrounding goal-conditioned learning problem

00:11:44.687 --> 00:11:48.955
Start with early-switch eligibility equal to zero Every segment in the

00:11:48.967 --> 00:11:53.367
toy model reaches the maximum length because we have excluded goal hits

00:11:53.761 --> 00:11:58.030
Increase eligibility while keeping competence fixed A second population

00:11:58.042 --> 00:12:01.915
of shorter segments appears in the survival curve The long tail from

00:12:01.927 --> 00:12:06.458
ineligible trajectories remains visible until the forced cutoff exactly

00:12:06.470 --> 00:12:11.181
as predicted by the mixture equation Next increase beta toward one Switching

00:12:11.193 --> 00:12:15.401
becomes more conservative early in the trajectory Increase the minimum

00:12:15.413 --> 00:12:19.407
length and watch the survival curve stay flat for longer before declining

00:12:19.861 --> 00:12:23.639
Finally raise competence Once checks become active a more competent

00:12:23.651 --> 00:12:27.740
agent tends to switch sooner These effects follow from the actual probability

00:12:27.752 --> 00:12:32.283
rule not from a hand-drawn animation Below the plot the target comparison

00:12:32.426 --> 00:12:36.372
holds the task transition constant Triggering a handoff changes the

00:12:36.384 --> 00:12:40.329
false-terminal target but leaves the continuation target alone This

00:12:40.341 --> 00:12:44.298
pairs the two lessons in one view scheduling changes the duration of

00:12:44.310 --> 00:12:48.232
collected segments while correct value learning keeps the underlying

00:12:48.244 --> 00:12:51.914
objective consistent The physical control problem and the collection

00:12:51.926 --> 00:12:54.532
schedule are allowed to evolve on different terms

00:12:56.479 --> 00:13:00.735
risk reframes a controller handoff as a decision about useful experience

00:13:01.142 --> 00:13:04.920
The success critic estimates competence on the current goal The scheduler

00:13:04.932 --> 00:13:09.236
uses that estimate to avoid spending every trajectory on already-mastered

00:13:09.248 --> 00:13:13.731
tails Its three modulation mechanisms keep the decision from collapsing

00:13:13.839 --> 00:13:18.119
into rapid back-and-forth switching that explores very little The mathematical

00:13:18.131 --> 00:13:22.674
foundation is just as important as the scheduler A handoff changes behavior

00:13:22.949 --> 00:13:26.512
but need not end the goal-conditioned return being learned Preserving

00:13:26.524 --> 00:13:30.182
that distinction keeps the critic's target from drifting with the scheduling

00:13:30.194 --> 00:13:34.044
rule We derived both the success target and the trajectory survival

00:13:34.056 --> 00:13:37.666
curve so that each part of the mechanism can be inspected independently

00:13:38.073 --> 00:13:41.863
The next lesson asks for a different kind of result Rather than proposing

00:13:41.875 --> 00:13:45.725
a practical switching heuristic and measuring its performance can we

00:13:45.737 --> 00:13:49.407
prove that an agent both learns a good policy and needs progressively

00:13:49.419 --> 00:13:53.556
fewer resets per unit of experience The answer will involve a constrained

00:13:53.568 --> 00:13:57.860
decision process two no-regret players and assumptions that are essential

00:13:57.872 --> 00:13:58.649
to the guarantee
